# claude-watch default cron entries — HOST / systemd deployment.
#
# This is the host analogue of container/cron.d/cw-default. Same jobs, same
# reasons; the only difference is that a host deployment has no fixed install
# prefix, so every deployment-specific value in this file is a @PLACEHOLDER@
# that gets substituted at INSTALL time.
#
# Install it with:
#
#     scripts/install-host-cron.sh              # render + install to /etc/cron.d
#     scripts/install-host-cron.sh -n           # dry run: print the rendered file
#     scripts/install-host-cron.sh --help       # all flags
#
# The installer resolves every placeholder from the local checkout (and lets
# you override each one), so this file itself stays free of any one operator's
# paths, usernames, or home directory. That is deliberate: this repo is public,
# and a second deployment (a different host, a different user, a different
# checkout location) must be able to consume this same fragment unmodified.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Placeholders
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   @CW_USER@       the user each job runs as. /etc/cron.d/ rows carry a USER
#                   column between the schedule and the command (user crontabs
#                   do not) — the cron daemon runs as root and setuid's into
#                   this account.
#   @CW_HOME@       that user's home directory. Only used to extend PATH; every
#                   command below is invoked by absolute path, because cron
#                   does NO variable expansion in the command field.
#   @CW_BIN@        absolute path to the claude-watch binary. See the hard rule
#                   below — this MUST be the same file the service execs.
#   @CW_STATE_DIR@  writable state dir (default /var/lib/claude-watch). Must be
#                   writable by @CW_USER@; consumers read from it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARD RULE: @CW_BIN@ must be the binary the daemon itself runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `claude_watch_build_info{version,commit,pr}` is emitted from COMPILE-TIME
# constants baked into the binary, so the gauge describes whichever binary CRON
# EXECS — never "whatever is running as the service". If cron and the service
# exec two different files, the build-identity gauge silently describes the
# wrong one, and nothing fails loudly: the metric keeps updating every minute
# with a stale commit.
#
# A host/systemd deployment can easily end up with two claude-watch binaries:
#
#   - the service's ExecStart, which runs the release build inside the
#     checkout (`<repo>/target/release/claude-watch`); and
#   - the on-PATH CLI copy that `make install` places in $BIN_DIR (~/bin by
#     default). The daemon is a build artifact, so `make install` copies it
#     rather than symlinking it the way it symlinks the script tools.
#
# Those two drift apart the moment one is refreshed without the other. Point
# this file at the FIRST one — the service's ExecStart path — which is exactly
# what `install-host-cron.sh` resolves by default.
#
# Do NOT "fix" the drift by symlinking $BIN_DIR/claude-watch into
# target/release/: `cargo clean` (or a profile change) leaves that link
# dangling and the on-PATH CLI simply disappears, and the next `make install`
# replaces the link with a copy again anyway. The supported fix is that
# `make deploy-systemd` depends on `install`, so a host deploy refreshes the
# $BIN_DIR copy and restarts the service from the same build.
#
# No fallback path is offered on purpose: if @CW_BIN@ is missing, the daemon is
# down too, and a silent fallback to a second binary would just re-create the
# drift this file exists to prevent.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATH
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Cron's default PATH is minimal (/usr/bin:/bin). Set an explicit one so
# /usr/local/bin and the user's own bin dir are reachable by anything the jobs
# below shell out to.
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:@CW_HOME@/bin

# Emit Prometheus-style metrics every minute into the node-exporter textfile
# collector dir. stderr goes to syslog under the `claude-watch-metrics` tag.
* * * * *   @CW_USER@ @CW_BIN@ metrics 2>&1 | logger -t claude-watch-metrics

# Publish the active-agents JSON state file (queue_id + alive per subagent)
# every minute. Consumers: the work-queue exporter (has_live_owner gauge), the
# queue mini-site (owner-status column), and any queue watchdog cron.
#
# Same single-binary rule as the metrics line above: this writes state that the
# daemon's own view is compared against, so it must not be produced by a stale
# snapshot carrying an older state schema.
* * * * *   @CW_USER@ @CW_BIN@ active-agents --json --write-state @CW_STATE_DIR@/active-agents.json > /dev/null 2>&1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional jobs (commented out — enable per deployment)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The container bakes these two as well. They are left commented here because a
# host deployment may already run an equivalent out-of-tree watchdog, and
# installing this fragment must not silently add event emitters to a host that
# did not ask for them. Uncomment whichever the deployment wants; the installer
# substitutes placeholders in commented rows too, so they are ready to enable
# in place.
#
# Stale-ready queue-item watchdog: every 5 minutes, scan for pending queue
# items that have been ready past the threshold and emit ONE aggregated
# `queue-stale-ready` event per qualifying tick (single-emit per queue id).
#*/5 * * * *  @CW_USER@ @CW_BIN@ stale-ready-check > /dev/null 2>&1
#
# Stuck / orphaned queue-item watchdog: every 5 minutes, emit `queue-orphaned`
# / `queue-stuck` events for running items whose owner PID is dead or whose
# heartbeat has gone stale. Emission is gated behind `[queue_check]
# emit_events` in config.toml (default false), so this runs the detector but
# stays silent until that toggle is flipped on.
#*/5 * * * *  @CW_USER@ @CW_BIN@ queue-check > /dev/null 2>&1
#
# Session-store rotation: once a day, bound the two unbounded growers under
# ~/.config/session/ -- prune old queue-logs/ transcripts (age + count cap)
# and roll completed-tasks.jsonl (size threshold -> dated gz segment, recent
# tail retained in the live file for the q-site DONE view). Idempotent; no-ops
# when nothing is over threshold. This runs the `session-task` Python CLI (on
# PATH via @CW_HOME@/bin after `make install`), NOT @CW_BIN@ (the Rust daemon),
# so it is a bare command name resolved off the PATH set above. Thresholds are
# overridable via ROTATE_* env vars (see `session-task queue rotate --help`).
#17 4 * * *  @CW_USER@ session-task queue rotate > /dev/null 2>&1
